 =?UTF-8?q?=20message=20that=20wakes=20somebody=20up?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A lead arriving today produces two emails. That is the whole notification
path. There is no SMS anywhere in this repository — `grep -rni twilio`
across every package.json and every source file returned nothing.

So the interval between a homeowner asking for a measure and anyone at this
business knowing about it is bounded only by how often somebody opens an
inbox. For home services that interval is the largest single determinant of
close rate, and it was the one stage of the funnel with no mechanism at all
— every other stage now has a ledger row and an event.

Wired at the three places a real lead actually lands: /api/leads,
/api/appointments, and both of the assistant's writing tools. A measure
booked by EcowoodsGuide at 23:40 is precisely the case email was never going
to cover.

NO NEW DEPENDENCY, DELIBERATELY

Twilio's REST API is an HTTPS form POST with basic auth. The npm package adds
a lockfile entry to a repository whose install expects the lockfile to be
current — a dependency added in a patch is a dependency that breaks `pnpm
install` on the machine applying it. Fifteen lines of `fetch` instead, with
a hard timeout and redirects refused. The cost is stated in the file: this
speaks Twilio's wire format, and changing provider changes this one file.

THE GUARD CAUGHT ME, AND IT WAS RIGHT

`pnpm seo:legal` failed:

    `twilio` is imported but no processor named like "twilio" is declared
    → It receives data. /privacy lists everyone who does, and a list that is
      missing one is not an oversight in a marketing page — it is an
      inaccurate statement about where a customer's information goes.

I was about to ship a feature that sends a customer's name and phone number
to a third party the privacy page did not mention. lib/legal.ts now declares
Twilio, /privacy renders it from the same list, and LEGAL_LAST_REVIEWED moves
to today. This is the guard doing the job it was written for, on me.

WHAT THE MESSAGE IS ALLOWED TO CONTAIN

An SMS crosses a carrier in the clear and lands on a lock screen anybody
standing nearby can read. So it carries the least that still lets someone
return the call: what happened, the name, the number, the city or postal
code, the service, and which page produced it.

It carries no email, no message body, no address, and nothing about the floor
they designed. That is enforced by the shape of the type rather than by
discipline — LeadAlert has no field for them — and a test asserts the shape
holds, because a future "just add the email so I can reply from my phone" is
exactly how a lock-screen leak gets written.

Provider errors log the STATUS and never the response body: an error body can
echo the request back, and the request contains a customer's phone number.

IT CANNOT BREAK A LEAD

Never awaited, never throws, hard four-second timeout. Unconfigured it
resolves quietly with one structured log line — most environments have no
credentials, and an alert path that shouted on every lead would be switched
off within a day and then be off on the day it mattered. Recipients must be
E.164 or they are dropped rather than handed to a carrier, and
ALERT_SMS_TO takes a list so a second estimator needs no deploy.

To turn it on: TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID, TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN, TWILIO_FROM and
ALERT_SMS_TO. Until all four are set nothing changes.

THE THIRD TEST NEEDLE THIS SESSION THAT HAD TO BE MADE CODE-SHAPED

"LeadAlert must have no `message` field" failed on the docblock for `phone`,
which reads "the entire point of the message". It matches a field declaration
now. Word-shaped needles keep finding prose; that is three for three.

Verified: 64 of 66 guards. verify:css needs postcss from node_modules, absent
here. verify:hygiene fails on a tracked patch file sitting on origin/main
from a web upload — HYG-02 on a real artifact, cleared when ship.sh applies.
parse-scan 616 files, zero diagnostics. Resolving typecheck clean. 36 new
assertions.

